  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nCooler temperatures and increasing chances for showers and\nthunderstorms return this weekend.\n\nCurrently, skies were partly sunny across western and central\nNorth Dakota with temperatures in the mid 80s to mid 90s. Strong\nsouth to southeast winds were also noted over the forecast area,\nwith gusts to around 45 mph. Widespread high thin clouds were\nsituated across the forecast area, with mid level cloudiness\npushing in from the south. High pressure at the surface and\naloft should keep us dry in the short term.\n\nLooking ahead to tomorrow. The upper level low over California \nwill push into the Rockies by Saturday. This will help pull in \nlow level moisture from the south. With the increasing moisture,\nwe will also see increasing low clouds lifting into the state \nfrom the south. A couple possible forecast problems Friday will \nbe temperatures and Fire Weather concerns. With the increasing \nlow stratus there has been a definite lowering trend for high \ntemperatures. The lowest forecast highs are expected to be in \nthe south central, where highs may not get out of the 70s. It's \npossible if the stratus is aggressive enough, highs in the mid \n70s are not out of the question along the SD border. As you go \nnorth into the north central, highs are forecast to reach the \nmid 80s. The highest temperatures in the state will be in the \nfar west and far east. Out west, highs are forecast to be in the\nupper 80s to lower 90s, which is a few degrees lower than the \npast couple of days. This should help limit fire weather \nconcerns. In addition, with moisture advecting northward, \nafternoon dewpoint temperatures are forecast to remain in the \n50s, possibly even mid to upper 50s, which will help to keep \nafternoon humidities in the mid and upper 20s. We will pass this\nalong to the evening and overnight shifts, but at this time we \ndo not anticipate Critical fire weather conditions in the far \nwest Friday, but will mention near critical fire weather \nconditions. \n\nOver the weekend the aforementioned upper low lifts from the\ncentral Rockies Saturday, into the northern Rockies by Sunday.\nThen into the northern High Plains Monday, before lifting into\nsouthern Canada by mid-week. With the upper low to our west, the\nweekend will bring on and of chances for showers and\nthunderstorms across western and central North Dakota.\nCurrently, SPC has a marginal risk for severe storms over far\nsouthwest North Dakota Saturday afternoon. Currently there is a\nlarge discrepancy between the NAM and GFS Cape values in the \nsouthwest Saturday afternoon. Both show mostly weak shear. \nBufkit soundings are not real impressive at this time, so \nconfidence is pretty low for the potential for severe storms \nSaturday afternoon. Will continue to monitor. As we get later \ninto the weekend and into early next week, temperatures trend \nlower, into the 70s and 80s so the probability for thunderstorms\nwill diminish Sunday and Monday. Then as we head into mid-week,\ntemperatures remain in the 70s and 80s, and with more of a \nquasi-zonal flow we would expect near daily chances for showers \nand thunderstorms.",
